dydo/understand/guard-system.md:168:- **PR-ready check**: a PR shows the branch as the remote holds it, so a PR-opening gh call is blocked while the tree it shows has staged, unstaged or untracked files, or HEAD has no upstream or differs from it (the local remote-tracking ref; no fetch). The calls are `gh pr create`, `gh pr new`, `gh pr ready`, a `gh api` POST to `repos/OWNER/REPO/pulls`, and `gh api graphql` naming `createPullRequest` or `markPullRequestReadyForReview`; a REST PATCH of `draft` cannot ready a PR and is not one. The line is read as its shell reads it: PowerShell for the PowerShell tool and on Codex for Windows, bash for Claude Code's Bash tool, both ways when unknown, and also as PowerShell when a bash line uses PowerShell syntax (a backtick line continuation, `&` starting a line, `$env:`); a reading that loses a `--head` another reading names adds no call of its own. A call counts at command position: after `;`, `&&`, `||`, `|`, a line break, a group or a subshell, past `VAR=value` words, shell keywords and `env`, `command`, `builtin`, `exec`, `nohup`, `time`, `nice`, `timeout`, `sudo`, `doas`, `xargs`, `stdbuf`, `ionice`, `setsid`, `unbuffer`, `caffeinate` and `wsl` (the same reading the dydo-command rules use), with gh under any path, case or `.exe` and gh's `-R`/`--repo` before `pr`. A commit message, a `grep` pattern, a comment or a heredoc body quoting one does not count, but a command substitution runs its command, so a `$(...)`, or in bash a backtick substitution, anywhere its shell expands it (inside double quotes too, not single) is read like a direct call. The tree is the call's directory: the tool input's `cwd`, else Codex's `workdir` (a relative one under the payload's `cwd`), else the payload's `cwd`, else the project root, as when Codex sends none, and the refusal says which. A call naming its branch (`--head [OWNER:]BRANCH`, `gh pr ready BRANCH`, a REST `head` field) is checked in every worktree with that branch checked out instead; it is refused when none has it, or when git lists one it cannot open (a stale, prunable entry). The refusal names every tree it checked, the clean ones marked clean. A call acting on a repository other than the tree's `origin`, by host, owner and name (case and `.git` aside; github.com when no host is named), is refused: run it from that repo's checkout. That repository is the one gh picks: a `gh pr ready` PR URL, else the last `-R`/`--repo`, else an inline `GH_REPO`, else an inherited `GH_REPO`, with `env -u`/`--unset GH_REPO`, `env -i` and an empty `GH_REPO=` applied left to right; for `gh api`, the endpoint's (its URL host or `--hostname`), where a lone `{owner}` or `{repo}` takes its part from `GH_REPO` or the checkout's origin and the literal part is compared. A directory outside git is allowed, wrapped call or not. It stops an accident, not a bypass, so it decodes nothing: a `pwsh`/`powershell -Command`, `bash`/`sh`/`zsh`/`dash`/`ksh -c`, `cmd /c` or `/k`, `eval`, `iex`/`Invoke-Expression`, `env -S`/`--split-string`, `ssh` (its remote command) or `watch` whose command string, read the same way, makes such a call is refused unread, asking to run gh directly so the check can see it, and so is any `-EncodedCommand`. So is a line the check cannot finish reading (a bare `case` word in a `$(...)`, an unpaired quote) when it holds a partial PR call, or holds a nested part (a `$(...)`, a group, an expanding heredoc body) and the word `gh`, `pwsh` or `powershell` anywhere in its text, under any path, case or `.exe`, since an `-EncodedCommand` hides gh's word; without one of them, such a line makes no PR call. Aliases, functions and other programs are not followed. A Codex argv-array command in its shell envelope (`[bash|sh|zsh, -c|-lc, script]` or `[pwsh|powershell, options, -Command, script]`) is its script, checked as a direct call; any other array is refused when its program is a wrapper holding such a call or any of its words reads as one. A help request is no PR: `gh pr create`, `gh pr new` or `gh pr ready` given `--help` or `-h` as a flag of its own prints gh's help and acts on nothing (gh 2.88.1), while `--help` as a flag's value (`--title --help`) or after `--`, or a real call later in the line, still counts. The refusal names what failed and asks for [`dydo pr-ready`](../reference/dydo-commands.md#dydo-pr-ready) there, whose `--body` also checks the body names its ticket and holds the reviewer's `PASS` block.
